Context menus: ignore the release that opened the menu

GtkModelButton treats an unpaired release — one with no matching press,
which is exactly the release of the press that opened the menu — as a
click. That is the X11 press-drag-release model: one press opens the
menu and selects from it.

Windows uses two steps. Releasing opens the menu and leaves it there; a
second, separate click chooses. GTK4 has no equivalent of GTK3's 500ms
guard, so the surprise is unconditional here.

Ignoring unpaired releases gives the Windows model. Ordinary clicks on
menu items, which do have a matching press, are unaffected, and so is
keyboard activation.

Applies to: gtk4 4.22.4

--- a/gtk/gtkmodelbutton.c
+++ b/gtk/gtkmodelbutton.c
@@ -1533,8 +1533,26 @@
                           GdkEventSequence *sequence,
                           GtkWidget        *widget)
 {
-  if (gtk_widget_contains (widget, x, y))
-    g_signal_emit (widget, signals[SIGNAL_CLICKED], 0);
+  /* An unpaired release is the release of the press that opened this
+   * menu: the pointer was elsewhere when the button went down. GTK
+   * treats it as a choice, which is the X11 press-drag-release model —
+   * one press opens the menu and selects from it.
+   *
+   * Windows takes two steps. Releasing opens the menu and leaves it
+   * there; a second, separate click chooses. Someone arriving from
+   * Windows lets go as soon as the menu appears and lands on whatever
+   * item is under the pointer.
+   *
+   * Ignoring unpaired releases gives the Windows model. Ordinary clicks
+   * on menu items do have a matching press and are unaffected, as is
+   * keyboard activation.
+   */
+  (void) gesture;
+  (void) x;
+  (void) y;
+  (void) button;
+  (void) sequence;
+  (void) widget;
 }
 
 static void
